inline_comments.body:
Thanks. Partly valid: I measured it before changing anything. On GB300 the old guard took 0.26 s per rank at TP4 and 1.24 s at TP2 (first call, including cuBLAS warm-up), with about 1 GiB of transient allocations from the stacked per-step outputs. That is high for what it is, but not a startup problem.

I kept checking every batch size from 1 to `max_reqs` rather than only the capture sizes plus a sparse set. The draft also runs eagerly or piecewise, for example above the largest capture size and on non-FULL paths, at any batch size, and the guard is about bitwise equality, where interpolating between sizes isn't safe. Instead I made it cheaper (eddf09a8ac):

- no host sync per size: mismatches accumulate in a device flag, read once;
- comparisons per step against `model.markov_bias`, without materializing all steps;
- `empty_cache()` afterwards.

On the real M3.1 Flash draft model at TP4, the whole `create()` (guard plus symmetric-memory setup) now takes 0.42 s, with a 367 MiB peak. The guard is now also stricter: real reference, peers' slices.
